# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1.2 have "desire_punish ∧ say_compelled ∧ gratified_any → lingers" and "lingers → generating"; mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1.3 has "desire_punish ∧ glad_enforce ∧ say_compelled ∧ gratified_any → lingers" and "lingers → generating"; they differ when only "it would always give us pleasure, and chime in with our feelings of fitness, that acts which we deem unjust should be punished", "when we think a person is bound in justice to do a thing, it is an ordinary form of language to say that he ought to be compelled to do it" and "we should be gratified to see the obligation enforced by anybody who had the power" are true, which the second allows
mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if (it would always give us pleasure, and chime in with our feelings of fitness, that acts which we deem unjust should be punished and when we think a person is bound in justice to do a thing, it is an ordinary form of language to say that he ought to be compelled to do it and we should be gratified to see the obligation enforced by anybody who had the power), then even here, the idea of the breach of what ought to be law still lingers in a modified shape"; mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1.3 does not
mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (it would always give us pleasure, and chime in with our feelings of fitness, that acts which we deem unjust should be punished and when we think a person is bound in justice to do a thing, it is an ordinary form of language to say that he ought to be compelled to do it and we should be gratified to see the obligation enforced by anybody who had the power), then even here, the idea of the breach of what ought to be law still lingers in a modified shape"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1: argue/verdicts: valid as mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1, mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r1.2 read it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict
